OpenAlloy NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

Copyright (C) 2026 Everton Vilhena Cardoso
See the end of this file for copying conditions.

Please report problems with the output of 'openalloy doctor' and
'journalctl --user -u openalloy'.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.6.2 (2026-10-31) [stable]

  The first run of the new automated tests on GitHub failed in the shell
  script check: the runner's older shellcheck flags two lines of install.sh
  (SC2015, 'A && B || C'). The lines were correct, but they are now plain
  'if' statements, and the CI installs a pinned shellcheck (0.11.0) instead of
  whatever version the runner's distribution ships. All the tests themselves
  passed on Python 3.10 to 3.14. No change in the program.

  Version 1.6.1 was tagged but not released.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.6.1 (2026-10-31) [stable]

** Repository and project files

  Automated tests on GitHub Actions for every push and pull request: the test
  suite on Python 3.10 to 3.14, the drivers against the emulated keyboards,
  ruff, shellcheck, the translation catalogs, the manual pages and the package
  build. The README shows the result, the latest release and the license.

  SECURITY.md: how to report a vulnerability privately, and what is in scope.
  Issue forms for bug reports (asking for 'openalloy doctor', 'openalloy
  status' and the service log) and feature requests; security reports are
  routed to private reporting.

  The project URLs (repository, issues, releases, changelog) are in the package
  metadata, and the README links to the latest release for downloading.

** Tests

  The test watchdog wrote to a fixed file, /tmp/openalloy-test-hang.log; when
  another user had created it, the whole test run failed to start. The file is
  now per user (/tmp/openalloy-test-hang-<uid>.log), never opened through a
  symbolic link, and a private temporary file is used if it cannot be opened.

** Test lab

  The USB/IP emulator of the test lab listened on every network interface
  (0.0.0.0), exposing the emulated keyboards to the local network while the lab
  ran. It now listens on the loopback only, which is all a QEMU guest needs;
  '--host' chooses another address. The lab is a development tool and is not
  installed; the program itself is unchanged.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.6.0 (2026-10-31) [stable]

** Documentation and packaging

  All documentation is now in English. The package follows the GNU layout:
  README.md, INSTALL, NEWS, ChangeLog, AUTHORS, THANKS and COPYING (the GNU
  General Public License, version 2). NOTICE became THANKS, CHANGELOG.md became
  NEWS, and the SparkyLinux first-steps guide became INSTALL.

  docs/ went from nine reports to three guides: PROTOCOL.md, DEVELOPMENT.md
  (architecture, design decisions, translations) and TESTING.md (test suite,
  test lab, results and the defects found in each audit).

  Copyright and authorship: Everton Vilhena Cardoso. Every source file carries
  an SPDX license identifier and a copyright line.

** New features

  Manual pages openalloy(1) and openalloyd(1). install.sh installs them for
  the user (~/.local/share/man/man1) and uninstall.sh removes them. The user
  service points to them (Documentation=man:openalloyd(1)).

** Bug fixes

  An effect parameter that is not a number ('apply wave -p speed=abc', 'nan',
  'inf') showed Python's untranslated text ("could not convert string to
  float", "cannot convert float NaN to integer"); it now says "speed: a number
  is expected", in the interface language. Found by the test lab.

** Changes

  tools/diagnostico_hid.py was renamed tools/hid_diagnostics.py.

  No other change in the protocols, the drivers, the commands, the settings,
  the presets or the service. Settings, presets and preset identifiers of earlier
  versions keep working unchanged.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.5.4 (2026-10-31) [stable]

  Defects found by the new test lab (lab/): the program installed by its own
  install.sh in Debian testing (Python 3.14, systemd 262, kernel 7.x) under
  QEMU, with the keyboards emulated as real USB devices (USB/IP) - real kernel,
  udev, logind and suspend.

** Bug fixes

  The udev rule 60-openalloy.rules never matched. It combined ATTRS{idVendor}
  (of the USB device) with ATTRS{bInterfaceNumber} (of the USB interface), and
  udev requires all ATTRS of one rule to match the same parent. On a clean
  system the user had no access ("no permission to open /dev/hidrawN"). The
  rule now uses udev's own usb_id builtin and matches on the hidraw node; it
  still grants access to interfaces 2 and 3 only - the keystroke interfaces 0
  and 1 stay closed. Tests reject the pattern that can never match.

  'openalloy release' and 'hold --release' after a 'hold' did not hand the
  lighting back to the firmware: in fixed mode the service lets go of the
  device, so the release had nowhere to send AutonomousMode. It now reopens
  the keyboard to do so.

  In "keyboard's own lighting" mode the service did not notice the cable being
  unplugged and plugged back (nothing is sent in that mode) and the status kept
  saying "connected". The device identity is now checked every second, as in
  fixed mode.

  On a slow or busy machine, replies from the service could never reach the
  window (a language change saved by the service but not applied, a frozen
  status): they were delivered at idle priority, and the animated preview never
  lets the GTK loop go idle. They now use the default priority.

  A lighting event that arrived before the initial data raised an IndexError in
  the terminal.

  'preset delete' of a preset that does not exist exited with status 0; it is
  now an error (status 1).

  A keyboard that refuses the vendor channel and has no LampArray logged the
  same line every 2 seconds, saying "using HID LampArray". It is now logged
  once, with the right wording.

** Test lab (lab/, not part of the installed package)

  A USB/HID emulator independent of the program's code (descriptors, vendor
  channel, LampArray per the HID standard, boot keyboard), a USB/IP server,
  injectable faults, a hex log, an SVG image of what the keyboard shows; a
  Debian forky VM with a disposable snapshot per run; scenarios for install,
  permissions, effects, faults, reconnection, real S3 suspend, the graphical
  interface, performance and uninstall; comparison between versions; a
  read-only tool to capture the descriptors of the real keyboard.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.5.3 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Fixes from the journal of the real HP keyboard (03f0:098f, firmware 21.16,
  SparkyLinux) and from the review of 1.5.2. No change in the LampArray
  protocol, 'hold', driver selection, settings, presets or commands.

** Suspend (systemd 256 and later)

  systemd freezes user services while sleep hooks run; the hook asked the
  frozen service to let go of the keyboard and waited for the reply. Effect:
  about 3 s more to suspend and 3 s more to resume, and both notices reached
  the service only after the resume ("going to sleep" and "resumed" in the same
  second). If they arrived in the reverse order, the service stayed "asleep"
  with the keyboard released until the next suspend.

  The hook no longer talks to frozen services (it only resets the USB device,
  as before); when it does talk, it waits at most 1.5 s and sends the time of
  the notice. The service ignores a sleep notice that arrives after the resume
  and notices the resume by itself from its clocks (CLOCK_BOOTTIME versus
  CLOCK_MONOTONIC), restoring the color or the effect. This also works with the
  old hook and with systemd versions that do not freeze.

** Stopping the service

  The SIGTERM handler called Event.set() and could deadlock against the main
  thread's Event.wait(); after a freeze the signal could also go to another
  thread and the main thread never woke up. Either way 'systemctl --user
  stop/restart' hung until systemd sent SIGKILL. Handlers no longer take locks,
  the main thread wakes every 0.5 s, SIGHUP reloads in a thread, and the
  handlers are installed before the socket exists.

  Stopping the service just as a connection arrived could end with "cannot join
  thread before it is started" and exit status 1; fixed, and a connection
  accepted during shutdown is closed.

** One language in the journal

  Core errors were translated when raised, so translated text appeared inside
  English log lines ("... refused ([Errno 71] falha de escrita em ...)"). They
  are now translated only when shown (window and CLI in the chosen language; an
  error already on screen follows a language change) and the journal is all
  English. The error number no longer appears twice.

** Internal RPC

  Each client's output queue was limited to 256 messages, so a burst dropped
  even a window that was reading normally. The limit is now 4 MiB of pending
  data; a reader gets everything, in order; a client that stopped reading is
  still disconnected.

** Command line

  'openalloy status | head -1' ended with "Error: Broken pipe"; it now exits
  quietly. An invalid number in a setting ('fps=abc', 'nan', 'inf') showed
  Python's untranslated text; it now says "a number is expected".

** Tests

  27 new tests (329 in total). Fixed sleeps before checks were replaced by
  waits with a deadline.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.5.2 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Maintenance from the external review of 1.5.1. No change in the USB
  protocol, RPC API, commands or data formats.

** Internal RPC

  An idle connection (silent, or with half a line) held a slot forever; it is
  now closed after 30 s. Replies and events could interleave on the same socket
  (a broken JSON line); each client now has a single output queue. Event
  subscriptions now end within about 0.5 s and reconnect when the service
  restarts. Stopping the service now shuts clients and the accept thread down;
  the listen backlog went from 16 to 64.

** Robustness

  A corrupted, deeply nested config.json crashed the service and every CLI
  command (RecursionError); configuration and preset files now have a size
  limit and are ignored with a warning when invalid. A corrupted
  'flash_validated' record crashed status(); it is now dropped. Deleting the
  same preset concurrently is now safe. Late replies from before a switch
  between service and window, or from a closed window, are discarded. A
  duplicated LampArray reply left one LED unidentified; the missing LED is now
  asked again. The CLI log no longer sticks to the stderr of the moment it was
  configured.

** Tests and tools

  25 new tests (302 in total). A per-test watchdog interrupts a hung test after
  120 s and writes all thread stacks to /tmp/openalloy-test-hang.log. A test
  that leaves OpenAlloy threads running fails. tools/test_install.sh OLD.zip
  NEW.zip installs, upgrades and rolls back with the real install.sh in a
  temporary HOME.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.5.1 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Audit of 1.5.0. Fixes and one internal optimization only.

** Bug fixes

  (high) A read error on the vendor channel (e.g. unplugged cable) became an
  UnboundLocalError/TypeError instead of a transport error: a local variable
  named '_' hid the translation function (a 1.5.0 regression). This could
  prevent the LampArray fallback and print a traceback in the CLI.

  (medium) The key reader of the reactive effects always ended with an
  exception when stopped (same cause) and kept its state "active".

  (medium) A translation with a wrong placeholder broke the message; the
  catalog compiler now leaves such a translation out and English is used.

  (low) '--lang' without a value showed the help of an internal parser; an
  unknown code was silently ignored and now warns. A runtime check of the
  audio module used 'assert' (removed by 'python -O').

** Optimization

  LampArray keyboards received 48 USB reports per second for an unchanged
  static color; refreshing identical frames is now per driver (LampArray 2 s,
  vendor channel 0.25 s as before): about 4 reports per second.

  10 new tests (277 in total); 9 of them fail on 1.5.0.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.5.0 (2026-10-30) [stable]

** Internationalization with GNU gettext

  Languages: English (default and fallback) and Brazilian Portuguese. Every
  user-facing text - window, command line (including help and argparse
  errors), service, error messages, effects, built-in presets, installer,
  uninstaller, menu entry and the diagnostics tool - is written in English in
  the code and translated through openalloy/locales/<lang>/LC_MESSAGES/
  openalloy.po (595 messages, 100% in both).

  Choose the language in Keyboard > Settings > Language (switches at once),
  with 'openalloy language pt_BR', 'openalloy --lang pt_BR ...',
  './install.sh --lang pt_BR' or OPENALLOY_LANG. The choice is saved and the
  service follows the window. The system language does not change the default.

  Plurals, message contexts, and numbers, percentages and dates in the format
  of each language. tools/i18n.py (pure Python): extract, update, compile,
  check, stats, all.

** US layout

  US (ANSI) is tested alongside ABNT2 (LED map, detection through LampArray
  and through the vendor channel); '--sim-layout ansi|abnt2|iso' simulates
  each. Built-in preset names are now English in the files (translated when
  shown); their identifiers ('matrix', 'por-do-sol', ...) do not change.

  47 new tests (267 in total).


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.4.2 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Merges the best of the parallel "1.4.1 HP-Driver" branch. RPC: 256 KiB per
  request, 24 clients and 48 concurrent calls; non-UTF-8 bytes give an error
  and the connection survives. LampArray: LED ids and RGBI values are validated
  before each report. Window: one status query at a time, nothing processed
  after closing, invalid preset index handled. A LampArray reply with a
  different but valid LED id is still accepted (rejecting it could stop a
  working keyboard from being recognized). 214 tests.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.4.1 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Audit and clean-up, no change in behavior. Dead code removed; 'doctor' and
  'brightness +/-' use the protocol constants; eight outdated screenshots
  replaced by two current ones (69% smaller project); the 'gui' extra declares
  pycairo.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.4.0 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Full code review, 50 new tests (200 in total). Fixed: an upgrade did not
  restart the running service; a fixed color did not come back when the
  keyboard was replugged on the same /dev/hidrawN; test-rgb from fixed mode
  handed the LEDs to the firmware; a layout change needed a replug; LampArray
  software brightness was lost on reboot; the LED color correction froze the
  preview; a hung window could freeze the lighting; stopping or starting the
  service from the window left it talking to nothing; invalid settings could
  loop the engine (now validated on save and repaired on load); invalid presets
  broke the list; the LampArray driver was not tried without permission on
  interface 2; audio capture did not recover from a PipeWire restart;
  malformed RPC requests dropped the connection; several CLI errors.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.3.1 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Red looked pink: screen colors (sRGB) were sent raw to LEDs, which are
  linear. Colors now go through gamma correction (2.2) before reaching the
  keyboard (live, fixed, hold and onboard). Setting 'led_gamma' (1.0 turns it
  off).


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.3.0 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Simplified interface: three tabs (Lighting, Keyboard, Logs), three main
  buttons (Apply, Fix on keyboard, LEDs off). The window follows the system
  theme. Fixed mode in the service: the frame is applied once and the device is
  released without handing the LEDs to the firmware; it is restored after a
  replug, a resume or a brightness change.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.2.1 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  New 'openalloy hold COLOR [-k KEY=COLOR] [--preset NAME] [-b LEVEL]' sets a
  color and exits without handing the LEDs back (HID LampArray);
  'hold --release' hands them back.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.2.0 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Support for the HP 03f0:098f with firmware 21.16 through HID LampArray (page
  0x59, the standard behind Windows 11 Dynamic Lighting). That firmware refuses
  the vendor channel on interface 2 (EPROTO already on query 0x10). The new
  hid_lamparray driver reads the LED list and each LED's key from the device,
  so the key map and the layout come from the keyboard itself. The driver is
  chosen automatically. Brightness is done in software there; onboard profiles
  are not available. The udev rule also opens interface 3 (lighting reports
  only). '--simulate-hp' simulates this keyboard. 138 tests.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.1.1 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  Onboard writes are locked until a live RGB test is confirmed on that unit
  (USB id, serial number and firmware); OPENALLOY_ENABLE_FLASH=I_ACCEPT_THE_RISK
  remains as an alternative. HID replies with an unexpected opcode are
  discarded; truncated replies cancel a read or write; a short USB write is an
  error. 120 tests.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.1.0 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  System suspend hook. Sound effects (equalizer, sound spectrum, sound pulse,
  music wave). A key-press reaction layer on top of any effect. Fire and snake
  effects. Identical frames are not re-sent more often than every 0.25 s. Nine
  new presets (19 in total). 111 tests.


* Noteworthy changes in release 1.0.0 (2026-10-30) [stable]

  First release: Linuity and PlasmaNGenuity combined with the Alloy Origins
  Core protocol (OpenRGB, hxoctkl). Live per-key RGB, writing to the three
  onboard profiles, GTK 4 interface, command line, systemd user service.


